<p>Canada announced a major push to strengthen its presence in the Arctic, with Prime Minister Mark Carney laying out roughly C$35 billion in spending to expand military capabilities and upgrade civilian infrastructure across the North. The plan pairs expanded military facilities with broader improvements to northern logistics—such as airports and other links that support year-round operations. Carney cast the move as both a sovereignty and security play in an increasingly competitive region, saying Canada can no longer rely on others. Beyond defence, the plan also leans into practical capacity-building for northern communities—aimed at making deliveries, emergency response, and travel more reliable in remote areas.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/CQgRtKLmtp1xfNEZmDUiDIL44LX_dv6AzO2mRiTfJFGN4sW5yICGwGDiwRkjyedt8i3YwGrLc1tXlGVM6v0_pwxWOZOHovzt1s67ySd-ApttkWQ2orVZTu2NWa1jdEisR23bJ5ep99XKxcO5Jx0TJBNN73RqXwEYvLieY6hRFJW6EFG4zRQ3UndxpBMlQ_SqKfhH_Q' alt='Canada’s prime minister, Mark Carney, visited the Royal Canadian Air Force 440 Transport Squadron, in Yellowknife, Northwest Territories, on Thursday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>New bases: Carney said Canada plans to build three bases in the Arctic as part of the expansion.</li><li>Icebreaker build: The package includes new icebreakers, expanding Canada’s ability to operate in Arctic waters.</li><li>Airfield upgrades: Carney’s plan includes expanding military airfields in the Arctic, with The Straits Times reporting C$32 billion for airfield expansion within the broader push.</li><li>Forward locations: Carney said the government will fund forward operating locations in the North to help assert sovereignty in the region.</li><li>Currency framing: Japan Today put the package at about $25.7 billion, while other outlets cited totals in Canadian dollars with slightly different rounding.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Prime Minister Mark Carney: Carney said Canada can no longer rely on others and wants 